  "grammarPoints": [
    {
      "id": "g01-01",
      "title": "Noun sentences with は and です",
      "sourcePages": "41–42, 44–47",
      "meaning": "Use a polite noun predicate to identify or describe the topic: X is Y. Japanese often leaves the topic unspoken when the situation makes it clear. Nouns do not take English-style a/an or plural endings; context supplies that meaning.",
      "formation": "(X は) Y です. は marks the topic and is pronounced wa in this use. です follows the noun or noun phrase.",
      "notes": [
        "は marks what the sentence is about, not necessarily its grammatical subject.",
        "The affirmative nonpast pattern is introduced here; noun negation and past forms appear in later lessons.",
        "Use names and titles naturally; speakers commonly omit a clear topic and address a listener by name or title rather than あなた.",
        "Use さん after another person’s name, never after your own. 先生 normally describes someone else; 教師 is a modest way to state your own teaching profession.",
        "Ages use 歳, school years use 年生, and clock times use 時; learn the counter with its pronunciation (四時 is よじ, 七時 is しちじ, 九時 is くじ). 二十歳 is はたち."
      ],
      "prerequisites": [],
      "examples": [
        {
          "english": "Mika is a new student.",
          "japanese": "ミカさんは新入生です。",
          "kana": "ミカさんはしんにゅうせいです。"
        },
        {
          "english": "My major is history.",
          "japanese": "専攻は歴史です。",
          "kana": "せんこうはれきしです。"
        }
      ],
      "schemas": [
