    {
      "id": "g01-02",
      "title": "Polite questions and question words",
      "sourcePages": "42–43",
      "meaning": "Turn a polite statement into a yes-or-no question with か. For a question-word question, put the needed question word in the corresponding noun or value position and keep the polite sentence frame.",
      "formation": "Polite statement + か. Wh-question: topic は question word ですか, or topic は question word + counter/time expression ですか.",
      "notes": [
        "A Japanese question normally does not need a question mark in standard printed text.",
        "Japanese は in the topic position is pronounced wa.",
        "Read 何 as なん immediately before です and these counters: 何時 (なんじ), 何歳 (なんさい), 何年生 (なんねんせい), 何番 (なんばん). Before many particles it is なに; 何人 meaning “what nationality” is なにじん.",
        "When answering, the listener can omit a repeated topic when it is obvious."
      ],
      "prerequisites": [
        "g01-01"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "english": "Is Ren a teacher?",
          "japanese": "レンさんは先生ですか。",
          "kana": "レンさんはせんせいですか。"
        },
        {
          "english": "What is your major?",
          "japanese": "専攻は何ですか。",
          "kana": "せんこうはなんですか。"
        }
      ],
